	"identifying_description": "Warps: 2-ply (medium brown and royal blue); wefts : 2-ply dark blue",
	"web_text": "For the Aymara people, the textile arts serve as their primary form of artistic expression. The simplicity of this ceremonial shawl exemplifies their aesthetic, which is characterized by the skillful and refined manipulation of yarns. While the surface of the cloth appears to be one solid color—indigo blue—the field is actually achieved by an overlay of differently hued yarns. The pre-dominate warp is spun of two-ply yarn, one deep blue, one medium blue; the weft, while hidden, is a dark brown. This layering gives the work a subtle radiance and a sense of depth. The ends are bordered with thin pink stripes and the edges are finished with the tubular binding known as rebete, the most beautiful and time consuming of their edging techniques.",
	"didactic_label": "For the Aymara people, the textile arts serve as their primary form of artistic expression. The simplicity of this ceremonial shawl exemplifies their aesthetic, which is characterized by the skillful and refined manipulation of yarns. While the surface of the cloth appears to be one solid color—indigo blue—the field is actually achieved by an overlay of differently hued yarns. The pre-dominate warp is spun of two-ply yarn, one deep blue, one medium blue; the weft, while hidden, is a dark brown. This layering gives the work a subtle radiance and a sense of depth. The ends are bordered with thin pink stripes and the edges are finished with the tubular binding known as rebete, the most beautiful and time consuming of their edging techniques.",
	"exhibition_history_text": "On the Grid: Textiles and Minimalism, de Young Museum, 7/23/16 - 2/12/17",
